<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quercy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quercy is a historic province of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cahors">Cahors</a>, roughly the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Lot and part of Tarn-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was contested between the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, the dukes of Aquitaine and the French crown, and it suffered heavily in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. Its limestone plateaux, the causses, and the Lot valley are known for wine and truffles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quercy.jpg" alt="Quercy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre Bona · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lacave-Chateau_Belcastel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As royal authority in West Francia weakened, the counts gathered rights over <b>Quercy</b>, Albi, Nîmes and Narbonne, and in the tenth century Raymond III Pons appears in some documents with the title of duke of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Paris of 1259 Henry III of England gave up his claims to Normandy, Anjou and Poitou and did homage to Louis IX for a duchy reduced to Gascony, or Guyenne, with promises of lands in the Limousin, Périgord, <b>Quercy</b> and Agenais.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Their lands stretched into <b>Quercy</b>, and the family survived among the competing lords of Aquitaine by playing English and French kings against each other.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-turenne">Viscounty of Turenne</a></li></ul></section>
